::ENG2_U05_UA_Q01::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.L.01
=A. Because the hallways are loud, students need a quiet place to study.#Correct. The introductory clause is punctuated correctly and the sentence is complete.
~B. Because the hallways are loud students need a quiet place.#Incorrect. A comma is needed after the introductory clause.
~C. Because the hallways are loud.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~D. Students need quiet, the hallways are loud.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q02::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.V.02
~A. quiet and loud#Incorrect. These are opposites, not derivations.
=B. precise and precision#Correct. Precision is derived from precise.
~C. school and bus#Incorrect. These are related by topic.
~D. helpful and noisy#Incorrect. These are not derivations.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q03::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.V.03
~A. awesome#Incorrect. Awesome is informal.
~B. cool#Incorrect. Cool is informal.
=C. beneficial#Correct. Beneficial is formal and precise.
~D. nice#Incorrect. Nice is vague.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q04::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.W.04
~A. The library is awesome and kids need it.#Incorrect. This is informal and vague.
~B. I want the library because I like quiet.#Incorrect. This is personal and not audience-focused.
~C. It should be open because that is nice.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=D. Extended library hours would provide students with a supervised place to complete assignments after school.#Correct. This is precise, formal, and audience-aware.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q05::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.V.03
=A. The feeling or association connected to a word.#Correct. Connotation is the emotional or cultural association a word carries.
~B. The number of syllables in a word.#Incorrect. Syllable count is not connotation.
~C. The punctuation after a word.#Incorrect. Punctuation is a convention issue.
~D. The source where a word appears.#Incorrect. A source is not the word's connotation.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q06::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.W.04
~A. Make it better.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
=B. Replace vague wording with specific language that identifies which students benefit.#Correct. This feedback gives a specific revision action.
~C. I do not like it.#Incorrect. This does not explain how to revise.
~D. Change everything.#Incorrect. This is too broad and unhelpful.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q07::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.L.01
~A. students cannot focus good#Incorrect. The error remains.
~B. students cannot focused good#Incorrect. This creates another error.
=C. students cannot focus well#Correct. Well correctly describes how students focus.
~D. students cannot good focus#Incorrect. The word order is incorrect.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q08::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.R.10
~A. Libraries often contain shelves.#Incorrect. This does not explain the student need.
~B. Some students like quiet places.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~C. Many schools have hallways.#Incorrect. This does not support the purpose.
=D. Students who wait for late buses need a supervised place to complete assignments.#Correct. This detail directly supports the purpose.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q09::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.V.02
=A. revisable#Correct. Revisable means able to be revised.
~B. visible#Incorrect. Visible is not part of the revise word family.
~C. visitor#Incorrect. Visitor is unrelated.
~D. river#Incorrect. River is unrelated.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q10::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.W.04
~A. I changed it because it sounded different.#Incorrect. This does not explain the improvement.
=B. I replaced "kids" with "students" because the audience is school leaders and the tone should be formal.#Correct. This connects the revision to audience and tone.
~C. I made it longer because longer is always better.#Incorrect. Length alone is not a revision goal.
~D. I removed the reason because feedback was difficult.#Incorrect. Removing the reason weakens the draft.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q11::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.V.03
~A. cool spot#Incorrect. This is informal.
~B. nice thing#Incorrect. This is vague.
=C. productive workspace#Correct. This is more precise and formal.
~D. stuff area#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q12::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.L.01
~A. The library is quiet, students can focus.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
~B. The library is quiet students can focus.#Incorrect. This is a run-on.
~C. Because the library is quiet.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
=D. The library is quiet, so students can focus.#Correct. The conjunction so correctly joins the complete thoughts.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q13::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.W.04
=A. Check conventions, word families, precise meaning, tone, sentence flow, and audience fit.#Correct. This combines the major Unit 5 skills.
~B. Check only spelling and ignore meaning.#Incorrect. Unit 5 synthesis requires more than spelling.
~C. Add difficult words even if they change the meaning.#Incorrect. Revision must preserve meaning.
~D. Remove all feedback and start a new topic.#Incorrect. Revision should improve the assigned draft.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q14::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.V.01
~A. The library thing is nice.#Incorrect. Thing and nice are vague.
=B. The proposed schedule change would support student academic progress.#Correct. Proposed, support, and academic are appropriate academic words.
~C. It is cool and stuff.#Incorrect. This is informal.
~D. Kids can do things there.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q15::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.V.03
~A. Denotation means feeling; connotation means spelling.#Incorrect. This reverses or misstates the terms.
~B. Denotation and connotation are both punctuation rules.#Incorrect. They are meaning terms.
=C. Denotation is dictionary meaning, while connotation is the feeling or association a word carries.#Correct. This accurately defines both terms.
~D. Connotation only applies to verbs.#Incorrect. Many types of words can have connotations.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q16::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.W.04
~A. The school should do this because I said so.#Incorrect. This is demanding and informal.
~B. It is nice for people.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~C. Kids want an awesome place.#Incorrect. This is informal.
=D. School leaders should consider extended library hours because the change would give students supervised academic support after school.#Correct. This is formal, precise, and respectful.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q17::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.L.01
=A. Students told teachers that the library should stay open later.#Correct. The sentence names the students, teachers, and library clearly.
~B. They told them that it should stay open later.#Incorrect. The pronouns are unclear.
~C. When students talked to teachers, they said it should stay open.#Incorrect. They is unclear.
~D. It should stay open because they need it.#Incorrect. Both it and they are unclear.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q18::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.V.02
~A. A plural noun#Incorrect. -ion does not simply make a word plural.
=B. A noun form or action/result#Correct. Revision and precision are noun forms built from related roots or bases.
~C. A past-tense verb#Incorrect. Past tense often uses -ed.
~D. An informal tone#Incorrect. The suffix does not automatically signal informal tone.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q19::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.V.03
~A. supervised#Incorrect. This is formal and precise.
~B. academic#Incorrect. This fits the formal context.
=C. awesome#Correct. Awesome is casual and weakens formal tone.
~D. beneficial#Incorrect. This is formal and precise.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q20::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.W.04
~A. Students do not need support after school.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
~B. The school should close the library earlier.#Incorrect. This changes the claim.
~C. The draft should discuss sports instead.#Incorrect. This changes the topic.
=D. Students who wait for late buses need a quiet place to complete assignments.#Correct. This keeps the meaning and makes the student need precise.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q21::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.L.01
=A. Students need a quiet place after school.#Correct. This sentence has a subject, verb, and complete thought.
~B. Because students need a quiet place.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~C. While students wait for buses.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~D. A quiet supervised place.#Incorrect. This is a phrase.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q22::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.W.04
~A. Choose random difficult words.#Incorrect. Word choice must fit meaning and audience.
=B. Identify who will read the writing and what that reader needs to understand.#Correct. Audience-focused revision starts with the reader and purpose.
~C. Delete every sentence with feedback.#Incorrect. Feedback should guide revision, not automatic deletion.
~D. Ignore tone and check only length.#Incorrect. Tone is important for audience fit.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q23::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.R.10
~A. The library should close before buses arrive.#Incorrect. The sentence supports extended hours.
~B. Students should stop riding late buses.#Incorrect. The sentence does not say this.
=C. Longer library hours would help students who remain at school until late buses leave.#Correct. This accurately paraphrases the sentence.
~D. Teachers should wait in the library.#Incorrect. Teachers are not the focus of the sentence.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q24::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.V.03
~A. However#Incorrect. However shows contrast.
~B. In addition#Incorrect. In addition adds another point.
~C. For example#Incorrect. For example introduces an example.
=D. As a result#Correct. As a result shows an effect or outcome.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q25::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.W.04
=A. Reread for meaning, audience, precise vocabulary, sentence flow, and conventions.#Correct. This strategy reflects the full Unit 5 synthesis process.
~B. Check only whether the draft is long enough.#Incorrect. Length does not prove quality.
~C. Add informal words so the tone sounds casual.#Incorrect. Tone must fit the audience.
~D. Replace every word with a synonym without checking meaning.#Incorrect. Synonyms can change tone or meaning.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q26::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q26
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.L.01
=A. When the library closes early, students lose a quiet place to work.#Correct. This revision adds a complete thought to the dependent clause.
~B. When the library closes early and.#Incorrect. This is still incomplete.
~C. When the library.#Incorrect. This is still a fragment.
~D. Library closes early when.#Incorrect. This is unclear and incomplete.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q27::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q27
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.L.01
~A. Some students ride late buses, they need a safe place to study.#Incorrect. This keeps the comma splice.
=B. Some students ride late buses, so they need a safe place to study.#Correct. The word "so" correctly shows the relationship between the ideas.
~C. Some students ride late buses they need a safe place to study.#Incorrect. This removes punctuation and keeps the run-on problem.
~D. Some students ride late buses, safe study.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q28::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q28
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.V.01
~A. The library is good.#Incorrect. "Good" is vague.
~B. The library is a place.#Incorrect. This is still vague.
=C. The library provides a quiet and reliable study space.#Correct. This revision uses precise wording that explains the library's value.
~D. The library is nice and stuff.#Incorrect. This is informal and vague.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q29::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q29
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.V.02
=A. build again#Correct. The prefix re means again, and construct means build.
~B. destroy completely#Incorrect. The word parts do not support this meaning.
~C. read silently#Incorrect. This meaning is unrelated to the root struct.
~D. speak loudly#Incorrect. This meaning is unrelated to the word parts.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q30::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q30
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.V.02
~A. contrast#Incorrect. This word looks similar but is not part of the construct word family in this lesson.
=B. construction#Correct. Construction shares the construct base and relates to building.
~C. constant#Incorrect. This does not share the same meaning connection.
~D. consult#Incorrect. This does not belong to the construct word family.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q31::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q31
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.V.03
=A. lonely and neglected#Correct. Forgotten suggests more than being empty; it creates a lonely, neglected feeling.
~B. bright and cheerful#Incorrect. The context creates an uneasy mood, not a cheerful one.
~C. crowded and noisy#Incorrect. The auditorium is empty, not crowded.
~D. ordinary and neutral#Incorrect. Forgotten carries a stronger feeling than neutral.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q32::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q32
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.V.03
~A. thin#Incorrect. Thin can be neutral.
=B. scrawny#Correct. Scrawny often sounds critical or negative.
~C. slender#Incorrect. Slender often has a positive or neutral connotation.
~D. lean#Incorrect. Lean can be positive or neutral.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q33::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q33
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.V.03
=A. observe#Correct. Observe means watch carefully to learn or understand, which fits the science context.
~B. ignore#Incorrect. Ignore has the opposite meaning.
~C. forget#Incorrect. Forget does not fit the task.
~D. decorate#Incorrect. Decorate is unrelated.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q34::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q34
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.V.03
~A. precise and exact#Incorrect. These are synonyms.
=B. vague and precise#Correct. Vague and precise have opposite meanings.
~C. observe and watch#Incorrect. These are related synonyms.
~D. analyze and examine#Incorrect. These are related synonyms.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q35::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q35
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.W.04
=A. The library should remain open later because students need a supervised place to complete assignments after school.#Correct. This revision adds a clear reason and uses a formal tone.
~B. The library is really good and stuff.#Incorrect. This is still vague and informal.
~C. Students need it because it is good.#Incorrect. This repeats vague wording.
~D. I like the library a lot.#Incorrect. This is personal and does not address the audience.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q36::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q36
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.W.04
~A. Make it better.#Incorrect. This is too vague to guide revision.
=B. Add a specific reason explaining how extended library hours help students who ride late buses.#Correct. This tells the writer what to add and why.
~C. I do not like it.#Incorrect. This does not explain what to revise.
~D. Change everything.#Incorrect. This is too broad and unhelpful.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q37::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q37
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.W.04
=A. Because some students ride late buses, they need a place to study after school.#Correct. Because correctly shows the reason relationship.
~B. However, some students ride late buses, they need a place to study.#Incorrect. However signals contrast and the sentence is awkward.
~C. Some students ride late buses, they need a place to study.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
~D. Students buses study because late.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q38::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q38
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.R.09
~A. fear#Incorrect. Cozy does not usually create fear.
~B. anger#Incorrect. Cozy does not suggest anger.
=C. warmth and comfort#Correct. Cozy has a positive connotation that creates a comfortable mood.
~D. confusion#Incorrect. Cozy does not suggest confusion.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q39::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q39
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.W.04
=A. The library provides students with a supervised academic space after school.#Correct. This revision is formal, precise, and fits school leaders as the audience.
~B. The library is awesome for kids.#Incorrect. Awesome is too informal and kids is less precise than students.
~C. The library thing is good.#Incorrect. This remains vague and informal.
~D. I really like the library.#Incorrect. This is personal and does not address the audience's needs.
}

::ENG2_U05_UA_Q40::
Question ID: ENG2_U05_UA_Q40
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG2.V.03
~A. cool#Incorrect. Cool is informal.
=B. beneficial#Correct. Beneficial has a formal, positive connotation that fits a proposal.
~C. awesome#Incorrect. Awesome is casual.
~D. neat#Incorrect. Neat is informal and vague.
}
